{
  "id": "names-not-numbers",
  "name": "Names, Not Numbers",
  "category": "services",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "xp": 200,
  "level": 6,
  "description": "The server answers by address but not by name.",
  "brief": "PC-01 can ping 10.0.2.10 perfectly well. Ask it for web.netquest.local and it gives up immediately.\n\nAn address needs no help. A name has to be turned into one first, and that is somebody's job — a job nobody has told PC-01 about.\n\nDNS-01 already holds the record. Point the clients at it.",
  "hints": [
    "Compare 'ping 10.0.2.10' with 'ping web.netquest.local' on PC-01.",
    "Run 'ipconfig' and read every line, not just the address.",
    "DNS-01 is at 10.0.2.53 and is already listening on 53/udp.",
    "Set the DNS server field on PC-01 and PC-02, then try nslookup."
  ],
  "explanation": "Nothing was wrong with the network — every packet could already get where it needed to go. What was missing was resolution: without a DNS server a host has no way to turn a name into an address, so it fails before sending anything at all.\n\nThat is why 'ping by address works, ping by name does not' is such a useful signal. It rules out cabling, addressing and routing in one step, and points straight at DNS.",
